@node Introduction
@chapter Introduction

@command{jaq} is a fast reimplementation of @command{jq} in safe Rust, and it is
widely described as a drop-in replacement. Measured against jq @value{JQBASE},
it is not one:

@itemize @bullet
@item
Nine jq command-line flags make jaq exit 2 with @samp{unknown flag}.
@item
Twenty-two jq builtins are @samp{undefined filter}.
@item
jaq does not auto-vivify. @code{null | .a = 1} and @code{@{@} | .a.b = 1} fail,
where jq builds the missing containers. This is the difference that breaks real
scripts, and it appears in none of the ``drop-in replacement'' write-ups.
@end itemize

An @code{alias jq = jaq} hides all of that until it fails at run time.

Pathfinder accepts jq's exact command line, translates it into jaq's, rewrites
the filter when a missing builtin needs supplying, and hands off. It implements
none of the jq language itself: jaq does the work.

@cindex scope
@cindex philosophy
Two rules govern what it will and will not do.

@table @strong
@item It will not silently change what a filter means.
Where jaq's semantics differ from jq's in a way that cannot be repaired from the
filter level---the assignment operators, @code{*}, @code{/}---Pathfinder
documents and warns rather than guessing.

@item It will not be more permissive than jq.
Names such as @code{leaf_paths}, @code{ascii}, @code{toarray} and @code{ANY} are
missing from jaq, but they are missing from jq @value{JQBASE} too. Defining them
would let a filter run here and fail on the next machine.
@end table

@node Installation
@chapter Installation

@command{jaq} must be reachable on @env{PATH}, or named by the
@env{PATHFINDER_JAQ} environment variable.

@section From source

@example
cargo build --release
./target/release/pathfinder --install-shim ~/.local/bin
@end example

This creates @file{~/.local/bin/jq} pointing at the binary. It refuses to
overwrite an existing @command{jq} unless @option{--force} is given: silently
replacing the system's JSON processor is not a decision a tool should make on
your behalf.

@section Declaratively

On a managed host, prefer the packaging under @file{packaging/}. The Nix
derivation takes a @code{withJqShim} argument, and the Guix package installs the
symlink in a separate phase; both default to @emph{not} claiming the
@command{jq} name, so adding the package to a profile never displaces an
existing jq by surprise.

The Nix build also pins jaq by store path rather than trusting @env{PATH}, which
removes the ``jaq is missing'' failure mode entirely.

Pathfinder never edits your shell configuration.

@node Invocation
@chapter Invocation

@section As @command{jq}

Exactly as jq. Every flag jq accepts is accepted, and unknown flags are rejected
with jq's own wording and exit code.

@example
jq -r '.items[] | select(.active) | .name' data.json
jq -s 'add' a.json b.json
jq -n --jsonargs '$ARGS.positional' 1 '@{"k":2@}'
@end example

@cindex safety
@cindex in-place
Flags are matched against an explicit whitelist and never forwarded blindly.
This is a safety property rather than tidiness: jaq has @option{-i}/@option{--in-place}
and jq does not, so a pass-through shim would turn @code{jq -i '.' data.json}
from jq's @samp{Unknown option -i} into a silent, irreversible rewrite of the
file.

@section As @command{pathfinder}

Under its own name, two further verbs are available.

@table @option
@item --explain
Print the translated jaq argv, the assembled program, which polyfills were
injected, whether the run will @code{exec} or pipe, and a warning if the filter
assigns to a path. Nothing is executed.

@item --install-shim @var{dir} [--force]
Create the @command{jq} symlink in @var{dir}.
@end table

These are deliberately unavailable through the @command{jq} symlink: a script
that passes @option{--explain} to what it believes is jq must receive jq's
@samp{Unknown option} error, or the drop-in surface is not jq's surface.

@node How It Works
@chapter How It Works

@section The two paths

@table @strong
@item exec
The process image is replaced by jaq's. There is no second process and no
copying; the cost over calling jaq directly is one @code{execve}. Verified with
@command{strace}: zero @code{clone} or @code{fork} calls, one successful
@code{execve} into jaq.

@item spawn and pipe
Used only for @option{-a}, @option{--seq}, and multiple input files, where jq's
behaviour cannot be expressed in jaq's argv and bytes must be reshaped.
@end table

The cost of @code{exec} is that Pathfinder cannot observe jaq's exit code or
stderr, and so cannot retouch either. In practice this costs nothing: the exit
codes already agree in every case tested.

@section Filter rewriting

Most filters are passed through byte-identical, which is what keeps the fast
path available. When a filter does need something, four things can be spliced
in, and their ordering constraints interact:

@enumerate
@item
@code{import}/@code{include} directives stay at the very top; jaq rejects a
directive that follows a @code{def}.
@item
The polyfill prelude precedes any use of the names it defines.
@item
A @code{$ARGS} wrap encloses the filter but sits below the directives.
@item
@code{$__loc__} is substituted in place, since it is a literal rather than a
function.
@end enumerate

@cindex line numbers
The prelude is emitted on the @emph{same line} as the filter's first line. jaq
reports errors against the text it was handed, so a prelude on its own line
would shift every reported line number by one; inline, the user's line numbers
are unchanged.

@cindex shadowing
A @code{def} of your own for a polyfilled name wins over the injected one,
because both jq and jaq take the last definition. Pathfinder therefore needs no
collision check, and @option{--explain} reports when it happens.

@section What gets polyfilled

Decided by evidence, not by the manual: each of jq @value{JQBASE}'s 226 builtins
was invoked against jaq @value{JAQVER} at its declared arity, and the 22 that
answered @samp{undefined filter} are the gap.

Supplied: @code{tostream}, @code{fromstream}, @code{truncate_stream},
@code{IN}, @code{INDEX}, @code{JOIN}, @code{trimstr}, @code{format},
@code{builtins}, @code{have_decnum}, @code{have_literal_numbers},
@code{input_filename}, and @code{$__loc__}.

Repaired---that is, shadowing a name jaq @emph{has}, because its behaviour
differs---@code{setpath}, @code{todate} and @code{todateiso8601}.

@cindex recursion
A repair must be self-contained: shadowing a builtin and calling it by its own
name inside the shadow is infinite recursion under jaq, not a call to the
original.

@node Divergences
@chapter Divergences

The full table, with measured evidence for each row, is in
@file{doc/DIVERGENCES.md}. In summary:

@table @strong
@item Auto-vivification
The one that matters. Calls to @code{setpath} are repaired; the @code{=},
@code{|=}, @code{+=} and @code{//=} operators cannot be, because they are syntax
rather than builtins. Work around it by seeding the container
(@code{.a //= @{@} | .a.b = 1}) or by using @code{setpath}.

@item Arithmetic corners
@code{"a" * 0} is @code{""} in jq and @code{null} in jaq; @code{1 / 0} errors in
jq and yields @code{Infinity}---invalid JSON---in jaq.

@item Diagnostics
jaq's error text and @code{debug} spacing differ. stderr is passed through
untouched so colour, interleaving and @code{debug} output stay correct.

@item Refused outright
@option{--stream}, @code{input_line_number}, the module-introspection builtins,
and @code{input_filename} across several input files. Each gets a diagnostic
naming the construct rather than a vaguer failure from jaq.
@end table

@node Testing
@chapter Testing

@cindex differential testing
Compatibility is established by differential testing, not by golden values. Each
case runs through real jq and through the shim; both must agree on stdout bytes
and exit code.

@example
make check     @r{# fmt + clippy + tests}
make diff      @r{# the differential suite alone, verbose}
@end example

Real jq need not be installed. The suite finds it via @env{PATHFINDER_REAL_JQ},
or fetches it ephemerally with @code{nix build nixpkgs#jq}, and skips if neither
is available.

This distinction is not academic. Hand-written goldens encode what the author
believed jq does---which is the very thing under test. Four such beliefs proved
wrong while this shim was being written: @option{--indent 0} does not mean
compact, malformed JSON input exits 5 in both (not 2 in jq), @code{ltrimstr} on
a non-string errors in jq @value{JQBASE} as well, and several names assumed to
be jq builtins are not builtins at all. Every one would have shipped as a bug.

